  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe central idea is to optimize the overlap integral by assuming a bi-symmetric structure in the function h(x). We enforce both symmetry around x=0.5 and about the interval [0,2], which halves the optimization space from 400 to 200 dimensions. This symmetry reduction is based on insights from discrepancy theory that often optimal solutions exhibit such periodicity and reflection properties. The algorithm projects parent solutions (where available) onto this symmetric subspace, ensuring that all candidates automatically satisfy the integral constraint while potentially exploiting high-quality parent regions in the optimization process. This approach differs from previous local refinement methods by leveraging constrained symmetry instead of unstructured local moves, which often isolates candidates to suboptimal regions.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Representation: h(x) is represented on n=400 grid points spaced by dx=2/400=0.005, with the symmetry h(i)=h(n-1-i) ensuring the integral constraint.\n-Objective: Minimize C5 = maxk \u222b[0,2] h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx with dx weighting.\n-Constraints:\n  - Symmetry: h(i) = h(n-1-i) for all i\n  - Integral: Sum_{i=0}^{399} h(i)*dx = 1 (derived automatically under symmetry)\n-Optimization: SLSQP with bounds [0,1] on each base variable and a single equality constraint on the sum.\n-Gradient: Sparsity pattern-inspired first derivatives using if-statements to handle indexing.\n-Gradient checks: L2 norm comparison between finite-differences and analytical gradients.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nPrecomputed parent projection:\n```python\nh_parent_sym = np.array(initial_h_values[:200])  # First 200 points\nfor i,v in enumerate(h_parent_sym):\n    n_400[i] = v\n    n_400[n_400-1-i] = v\nintegral_proj = np.sum(n_400) * dx  # Must equal 1 by symmetry\n```\n\nCore stochastic local search:\n```python\ndef build_h(x):\n    h_vec = np.zeros(400, dtype=float)\n    for j in range(200):\n        h_vec[j] = x[j]\n        h_vec[399-j] = x[j]  # Symmetric index\n    return h_vec\n\ndef objective(x):\n    h_vec = build_h(x)\n    corr = np.correlate(h_vec, 1.0-h_vec, mode='full')\n    max_corr = np.max(corr) * dx\n    return max_corr\n\ndef constraint(x):\n    return np.sum(x) - 100  # For dx=0.005 and integral=1\n\nbounds = [(0.0,1.0)]*200\nconstraints = {'type':'eq','fun':constraint}\n\n# Build base point explicitly\nparams = np.array(initial_h_values[:200], dtype=float) * (100/sum(initial_h_values[:200]))  # Scale preserve symmetry\n\ndef grad_objective(x):\n    h_vec = build_h(x)\n    c = np.correlate(h_vec,1-h_vec,mode='full')\n    dcorr_dparams = np.zeros(200)\n    # Sparse gradient calculation using symmetry\n```\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n- Base variation: Single projection of parent solution with optional perturbation (normal/Uniform)\n- Enhance diversity: Add sinusoidal components (0.01*BasePeriodic) to each base point\n- Optimization engine: PySFLB with adaptive line search andrestarts\n- For large budget: Hierarchical refinement using Curtius scaling and waveform expansion\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n- Initial projection: 0.1 seconds\n- Individual optimization: 200 variables in SLSQP normally ~100 function calls\n- Time monitoring: Evaluate new candidates separately if Objective exceeds previous best\n- Fallback: Use projected descent from best_point if major solver failures occur\n- Memory use: Maintains all 10 best candidates concurrently\n- Budget usage: 200-Sec warmup (projection and constraint config), 500-s time for stochastic search\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Explicit arithmetic error-checking: 1<=objective<=numerically detected bounds\n- Objective re-validation: Periodically (max 40 candidates) re-score with independent correlation\n- Feasibility checks: Confirm domain (0<=h[i]<=1) and integral = 1 dx after each candidate\n- Periodic (20% time) projection to full grid to allow external comparison scripts:\n```python\ndef validate_solution(x):\n    return (\n        all(0.0 <= v <= 1.0 for v in build_h(x)),\n        np.linalg.norm(np.sum(build_h(x)) * dx - 1) < 1e-6\n    )\n```",
